namespace redis;

-- Shared cache and rate-limit state for JWC apps.
--
-- This package carries no code, and that is the whole design in 1.0.
-- `redis.get`, `set`, `del`, `incr`, `expire`, `rate_limit` and `enabled`
-- are provided by the compiler (builtins.md §8), over the driver in
-- `jwc-lang`'s `src/redis_engine.rs`. What the package supplies is the
-- name: `import redis;` resolves against a `dependencies` entry (names
-- §6.2.1), and a package import is what brings the namespace into the
-- declaration space (§6.2.3). Without this manifest the import is
-- `E0201: unknown import`.
--
-- The reason the surface is not written here is that it cannot be. RESP
-- is a binary protocol over TCP and JWC has no socket, so the driver has
-- to be Rust — `ecosystem.md` calls this the core tier and gives the
-- integration template. A pure-JWC layer on top would only be able to
-- rename what the compiler already exposes.
--
-- There is no in-process fallback. The 0.9.x version of this package fell
-- back to the `cache_*` built-ins when Redis was unconfigured; those are
-- gone from the 1.0 vocabulary, and the fallback was the wrong default
-- anyway. Sharing is the entire point: a per-process rate limiter behind
-- two replicas admits twice the limit and nothing in the response says
-- so. Without a server, every name except `enabled()` raises.
--
--   if (redis.enabled()) { ... }        -- when the call is optional
--   let n = redis.incr($key);           -- when it is not: let it raise
